99 = 331 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 433 = 3 \\cdot 331 \\cdot 433$.\n\nNone of these are perfect squares, so $d = \\sqrt{3 \\cdot 331 \\cdot 433}/140 = \\frac{\\sqrt{429969}}{140}$.\n\nLet me double-check my computation of $d^2$.\n\nActually, let me recompute more carefully.\n\n$d^2 = \\frac{(3r_1+r_2)(r_1+3r_2)}{3}$\n\n$3r_1 + r_2 = 3\\cdot\\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{363}{140} = \\frac{9}{2} + \\frac{363}{140}$\n\n$\\frac{9}{2} = \\frac{630}{140}$\n\n$\\frac{630+363}{140} = \\frac{993}{140}$\n\n$r_1 + 3r_2 = \\frac{3}{2} + 3\\cdot\\frac{363}{140} = \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{1089}{140}$\n\n$\\frac{3}{2} = \\frac{210}{140}$\n\n$\\frac{210+1089}{140} = \\frac{1299}{140}$\n\n$d^2 = \\frac{993 \\cdot 1299}{140 \\cdot 140 \\cdot 3} = \\frac{993 \\cdot 1299}{3 \\cdot 19600}$\n\n$993/3 = 331$, so $d^2 = \\frac{331 \\cdot 1299}{19600}$.\n\n$331 \\cdot 1299$: $331 \\cdot 1000 = 331000$, $331 \\cdot 299 = 331\\cdot300 - 331 = 99300 - 331 = 98969$. Total $331000 + 98969 = 429969$.\n\nSo $d^2 = \\frac{429969}{19600}$.\n\n$429969 = 3 \\cdot 143323$. $143323 / 331 = 433$. So $429969 = 3 \\cdot 331 \\cdot 433$.\n\nSo $d = \\frac{\\sqrt{429969}}{140} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3 \\cdot 331 \\cdot 433}}{140}$.\n\nHmm, this doesn't simplify to a nice number. Let me re-examine whether I have the right formula and the right interpretation.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. The problem says \"the length of the External Bridge is exactly twice the length of the Internal Bridge.\" So $L_{ext} = 2 L_{int}$.\n\nThe standard formulas for common tangent lengths between two circles (which is what this is, even though they're spheres—the tangent segment between two spheres lies in the plane containing both centers, so it reduces to the 2D circle problem):\n\n- External tangent length: $\\sqrt{d^2 - (r_1 - r_2)^2}$\n- Internal tangent length: $\\sqrt{d^2 - (r_1 + r_2)^2}$\n\nThese require $d > r_1 + r_2$ for the internal tangent to exist (non-degenerate).\n\nSo $L_{ext} = 2L_{int}$ gives:\n\n$d^2 - (r_1-r_2)^2 = 4(d^2 - (r_1+r_2)^2)